   "id": "d30b2a7d5c65",
   "title": "DEFRAG: Archive of Synthetic Dreams",
   "start": "2026-11-19T18:00-07:00",
   "end": "2026-11-19T23:00-07:00",
   "venue": "The Permanent, 330 W Pender St, Vancouver, BC V6B 1T1, Canada",
   "city": "Vancouver, BC",
   "price_text": "$30 cad",
   "is_free": false,
   "description": "DEFRAG: Archive of Synthetic Dreams November 18–19, 2026 · The Permanent · Vancouver Two days at a glance Wednesday, November 18 2–5 p.m. · Creative AI presentations & demos 7–10 p.m. · Exhibition preview 8 p.m. · Ways of Something, Episode 1 screening Thursday, November 19 6–11 p.m. · Exhibition & live program Artist talks, live audiovisual performances & Misagh Azimi’s closing AV set. All times local to Vancouver. Venue closed November 18, 5–7 p.m. Full program below. The archive remembers. The machine dreams. DEFRAG brings the fragments together. DEFRAG brings artists, researchers and audiences together through moving images, generative installations, live audiovisual performance and creative AI workshops. In computing, defragmentation gathers scattered pieces of data. Here, it becomes a way to think about cultural memory: images, bodies, sounds and histories broken apart and recombined through digital systems. What do we preserve, what do machines invent, and what emerges between the two? Presented by Ministry of Next, in collaboration with SFU’s Metacreation Lab and Internet Archive Canada, inside The Permanent—home to servers preserving the internet’s past. Wednesday, November 18 — Creative AI Presentations, Exhibition Preview + Screening An afternoon of knowledge sharing around Open Source tools for ethical creative AI, alongside a preview of the exhibition. These are free tools for generating visuals or audio all locally and without using models trained on stolen data. All times local to Vancouver. 2–2:10 p.m. Welcome and introduction 2:10–2:50 p.m. AutoLume visual synthesizer. https://www.metacreation.net/autolume 2:50–3:20 p.m. Image generation by model bending and training with Ahmed Abuzuraiq Kiln · ComfyUI Model Bending 3:20–3:40 p.m. Break 3:40–4:15 p.m. Fragmenta audio generation with Misagh Azimi https://github.com/MAz-Codes/Fragmenta 4:15–4:50 p.m. MIDI-GPT for composition with Paul Triana https://github.com/Metacreation-Lab/midigpt-LIVE 4:50–5 p.m. Questions and closing discussion 5–7 p.m. Dinner break — venue closed; doors reopen at 7 p.m. 7–8 p.m. Exhibition preview 8–9 p.m. Introduction and screening: Ways of Something, Episode 1, compiled by Lorna Mills with participating artists 9–10 p.m. Exhibition preview continues 10 p.m. Close Thursday, November 19 — Exhibition + Live Program 6:00–11:00 p.m. 6–7 p.m. Doors open · Exhibition viewing 7–7:10 p.m. Welcome and curatorial introduction 7:10–7:45 p.m. Short artist talks + audience Q&A 7:45–7:50 p.m. Break 7:50–8:10 p.m. Carl Faia — Shelley’s Dream — Study I: A Strange Multiplicity of Sensations 8:10–8:30 p.m. Exhibition viewing and performance changeover 8:30–9:15 p.m. M and the Apostles — collaborative audiovisual performance Erica Lapadat-Janzen, Philippe Pasquier, Paul Triana and Misagh Azimi Visuals by Erica Lapadat-Janzen. 9:15–9:30 p.m. Transition with background music 9:30–11 p.m. AV set by Misagh Azimi 11 p.m. Close Tickets All prices in Canadian dollars. Early bird: $25 per day / $40 two-day pass Regular: $30 per day / $50 two-day pass Supporter: $75 two-day pass, with an additional contribution toward artist fees and production Student and community discounts available upon request. Location The Permanent 330 West Pender Street, Vancouver, BC For accessibility questions, contact studio@theministryofnext.com. Curated and produced by Erica Lapadat-Janzen / Ministry of Next. Developed with SFU’s Metacreation Lab and Internet Archive Canada, with support from the FCAT Knowledge Mobilization Activity Fund, Alliance Française and BC+AI Ecosystem Association. Meet the artists Explore the full artist bios and exhibition guide ↗ Artwork + Performance Descriptions Dreamscape Erica Lapadat-Janzen and Philippe Pasquier · 2024 Developed from Erica Lapadat-Janzen’s personal image archive using Autolume, Dreamscape transforms self-portraiture and internet-native imagery into a slowly shifting synthetic dream space. Working with a small, artist-curated dataset, the project explores identity, embodiment and the unstable boundary between human recollection and computational reconstruction. Ensemble Arshia Sobhan and Philippe Pasquier Ensemble reinterprets the Persian calligraphic practice of siyah-mashq through a small, manually crafted dataset and a model trained from scratch. Looping calligraphic forms and soundscapes derived from the reed pen explore cultural knowledge, authorship and continuity. SlowCore Bingmiao Zheng and Philippe Pasquier SlowCore is an audio-reactive generative AI artwork in which surreal architectural environments evolve with musical input. A custom-trained StyleGAN2 model translates rhythm, frequency and intensity into shifting space, colour and atmosphere. Music production and sound design: Monobor. Model of Me Carla Gannis · 2026 · Three-channel video · 5 min 36 sec Model of Me stages an encounter between Carla Gannis and her AI alter ego, C.A.R.L.A. G.A.N., inside an architectural structure shaped like an inverted woman’s head. Rooted in physical sculptures and animated through AI, the work questions human-centred models of intelligence through unfamiliar senses, pain and mortality. Original soundtrack: R. Luke DuBois. Am I the Machine? Am I the Home? Carla Gannis, in collaboration with Cleveland Adams · 2024 · Single-channel video · 1 min 45 sec A transparent female head houses a hybrid dwelling of obsolete technological components and organic forms. Moving between AI-generated imagery and physical models, the work explores consciousness, domesticity and the boundaries between technological augmentation and handmade craft. Research and animation assistance: Yesha Shah and Marilyn Fang. Petting Zoo: Epic Biblical Lorna Mills · 2020 Found and altered animated GIF collage on video. Ways of Something, Episode 1 Compiled by Lorna Mills with participating artists Rollin Leonard’s contribution to Ways of Something, Episode 1. Image from the official project website. Explore Ways of Something ↗ Ways of Something reworks John Berger’s 1972 television series Ways of Seeing through contributions from networked artists. DEFRAG presents Episode 1, bringing the project’s collective approach to image-making into the exhibition’s conversation about archives, remix and visual culture. Shelley's Dream — Study I: A Strange Multiplicity of Sensations Carl Faia · Live audiovisual performance · Approximately 15 minutes Carl Faia takes a passage from Mary Shelley’s Frankenstein as the sole material for a live audiovisual study, breaking apart and reassembling spoken language through generative sound and image. Developed during his Artificial Muse residency, the work moves from fragmented sensation toward coherence. M and the Apostles November 19 · 8:30–9:15 p.m. M and the Apostles brings Erica Lapadat-Janzen, Philippe Pasquier, Paul Triana and Misagh Azimi together for a collaborative live audiovisual performance, with visuals by Erica Lapadat-Janzen. Moving images and electronic sound meet in a shared exploration of fragments, archives and synthetic dreams. Visual study for M and the Apostles. Live visuals by Erica Lapadat-Janzen. Misagh Azimi — AV set November 19 · 9:30–11 p.m. Misagh Azimi closes DEFRAG with an audiovisual set drawing on his work between techno, electronica and digital media. His electronic music project, Nullen, combines driving beats, textural layers and dark sequences to create atmospheric, physical experiences. Promotional image supplied by the artist. © Misagh Azimi. Artist website ↗ · Nullen ↗ Venue partner",
