   "description": "🤖 AGENTS, BENCHMARKS & THE OPEN WEB 🤖 Join us at Northeastern University on Tuesday, November 24 for our last regular meetup of the year. This month we're getting practical about AI-assisted coding: sandboxes where agents can check their own work, which models actually handle real Git work, and a live demo of agents shipping code through git. Plus a look at how modern HTML and CSS are replacing JavaScript. And pizza, and plenty of time to hang out with the Vancouver JS crowd. 🎙️ Featured Talks: Shawn McKay (Sr Staff Engineer @ Amplitude, DevX lead): Sandboxes for AI Agents. Building sandboxes where agents can check their own work, from seven designs and their trade-offs to where to start. Chris Griffing (Developer Advocate @ GitKraken): Get Benched with GitBench. A recap of GitBench, the benchmark for how well AI models handle real Git tasks: how it was built, how to read the results, and what jumps out of the stats. Zach Jensz (Software Engineer & conference speaker): HTML and CSS are eating JS alive. Modern web APIs like popover, anchor positioning and even squircles are replacing custom JS implementations. A glimpse into cutting your bundle size and maintenance burden. Mike Nikles (building Moru): Agents write the code, git keeps the receipts (live demo). A Claude Code agent takes a request, submits a change, gets reviewed by another agent, fixes CI failures and keeps iterating until green, with every revision signed and recorded in git next to the code. 📅 Schedule: 5:30–6:00pm: Arrive and grab a slice 🍕 6:00–7:20pm: Talks & presentations 🎤 7:20–8:00pm: Mingle and network 🤝 💸 Interested in sponsoring an event? Reach out to us on LinkedIn. 🎤 Got a 5–15 min talk idea? Submit your proposal on our GitHub. Come for the JavaScript, stay for the community. 💛",
