   "id": "997821536f86",
   "title": "Vancouver Systems #6",
   "start": "2026-11-24T19:00-07:00",
   "end": "2026-11-24T22:00-07:00",
   "venue": "Vancouver, BC",
   "city": "Vancouver, BC",
   "price_text": "Free",
   "is_free": true,
   "description": "https://vancouver.systems #6 Join us for two talks on systems programming: Welcome to the Vulnerability Industrial Complex by Michael Klishin How does one small team of open source maintainers cope with a two orders of magnitude more security vulnerability report per month increase vs. just one year ago? Bloom filters by Vladimir Nikolic Bloom filters are a category of probabilistic set data structures. I.e. they tell us whether an item is present or not, and optionally in what count. Probabilistic means that queries don't give exact answers, but could be slightly erroneous in a fraction of the queries. In many cases, this is sufficient! And what we gain is an enormous improvement in memory usage, often multiple orders of magnitude. This talk will show a simple example of a Bloom filter, and then the many variants that exist that optimize for speed, or add additional functionality such as item count or removal. The talk also covers the configurable parameters and the guarantees one gets from Bloom filters. Systems folks will appreciate the efficiency and ubiquity of Bloom filters -- they're used anywhere from browsers, to databases, to scientific software. Drinks purchasable at the bar. Light snacks will be provided. Please eat dinner before the event.",
